# Freeness modulo a nilpotent ideal

Let `R` be a commutative unital ring, `I ⊆ R` an ideal, `M` an `R`-module, `A` a set and
`(x_α)_{α ∈ A}` a family of elements of `M`. Assume that `I` is nilpotent, that the family
of images `(x̄_α)_{α ∈ A}` is a basis of the `R/I`-module `M/IM`, and that
`Tor₁^R(R/I, M) = 0`. Then `M` is free on `(x_α)_{α ∈ A}` over `R`, that is,
`(x_α)_{α ∈ A}` is an `R`-basis of `M`.

## Notes on the formalization

Being a basis, or free on a family, is the standard notion: the family is linearly
independent and spans. The conclusion is expressed as the conjunction
`LinearIndependent R x ∧ ⊤ ≤ Submodule.span R (Set.range x)`, which is exactly what
`Module.Basis.mk` needs in order to produce a `Module.Basis A R M` whose underlying
function is `x`. The index set `A` may be infinite, so linear combinations are always
finitely supported, which is built into `LinearIndependent` and `Submodule.span`.

The quotient module `M/IM` is `M ⧸ (I • ⊤)`, where `I • (⊤ : Submodule R M)` is the
submodule generated by all `a • m` with `a ∈ I` and `m ∈ M`; it carries an
`(R ⧸ I)`-module structure automatically.

The hypothesis `Tor₁^R(R/I, M) = 0` is exactly the statement that the multiplication map
`I ⊗_R M → M`, `a ⊗ m ↦ a • m`, is injective, and that equivalent injectivity
formulation is the one adopted here.
-/
